1
00:00:00,133 --> 00:00:02,733
The biggest misunderstanding people have about drinking tea

2
00:00:02,966 --> 00:00:04,700
is thinking tea is just a beverage

3
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:05,633
It's not

4
00:00:05,966 --> 00:00:07,766
In Chinese relationships

5
00:00:07,766 --> 00:00:10,433
tea is often not for quenching thirst

6
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:12,333
It's for testing distance

7
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:14,966
and for putting a price tag on a relationship

8
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:18,233
You think someone invites you for tea because they're thirsty

9
00:00:18,566 --> 00:00:21,333
But often, what really matters isn't the tea itself

10
00:00:21,633 --> 00:00:23,933
It's whether you're willing to sit down

11
00:00:24,133 --> 00:00:25,900
whether you're willing to give them your time

12
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:27,933
whether you're willing to hear them out

13
00:00:28,033 --> 00:00:30,966
That's the most powerful thing about a cup of tea

14
00:00:30,966 --> 00:00:32,933
It's not as direct as money

15
00:00:33,166 --> 00:00:35,000
and not as cold as a contract

16
00:00:35,166 --> 00:00:36,233
It's gentle

17
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:37,366
Dignified

18
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:39,833
It might even look like nothing happened at all

19
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,366
But how close or distant a relationship is, how important it is, whether it's worth continuing

20
00:00:44,533 --> 00:00:47,166
often gets re-evaluated right there in that one cup of tea

21
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:50,000
So this video isn't about tea culture

22
00:00:50,166 --> 00:00:51,900
or tea knowledge

23
00:00:52,033 --> 00:00:54,033
It's about one counterintuitive insight

24
00:00:54,233 --> 00:00:55,633
Tea is not a beverage

25
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:58,633
Tea is the gentlest form of social currency in Chinese culture

26
00:00:58,766 --> 00:01:01,233
What it really exchanges isn't tea liquid

27
00:01:01,333 --> 00:01:04,633
but time, face, trust, and favors

28
00:01:04,733 --> 00:01:05,900
Have you ever noticed

29
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:08,533
Chinese people rarely say things directly

30
00:01:08,766 --> 00:01:09,766
Want to discuss a collaboration

31
00:01:09,966 --> 00:01:11,366
they might not send a proposal first

32
00:01:11,533 --> 00:01:12,600
they might just say

33
00:01:12,933 --> 00:01:14,100
let's grab tea sometime

34
00:01:14,333 --> 00:01:15,733
Want to smooth things over

35
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:17,033
they might not apologize first

36
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:18,366
they might just say

37
00:01:18,633 --> 00:01:20,700
come over, let's brew some tea

38
00:01:20,933 --> 00:01:22,000
Want to ask for a favor

39
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:23,700
they might not bring it up right away

40
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:25,633
they might just slide the teacup over first

41
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:27,000
make some small talk first

42
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:28,600
ask how you've been lately

43
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:30,566
This whole routine seems roundabout

44
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:32,133
but it's not pointless

45
00:01:32,333 --> 00:01:34,833
It's reducing the awkwardness cost in the relationship

46
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,300
Some things are too direct to handle

47
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:39,100
Like

48
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:40,333
Can you help me

49
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:42,000
Are you willing to take my side

50
00:01:42,133 --> 00:01:43,400
Do you actually respect me or not

51
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:45,400
Once these words leave your mouth

52
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:46,833
the other person either has to say yes

53
00:01:47,033 --> 00:01:48,100
or say no

54
00:01:48,233 --> 00:01:49,566
and the mood turns stiff

55
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:53,333
Tea's role is to give both sides a buffer zone

56
00:01:53,433 --> 00:01:54,800
You can sit down first

57
00:01:54,933 --> 00:01:55,833
take a sip first

58
00:01:56,033 --> 00:02:00,133
chat about the weather, catch up, talk about the kids, talk about work

59
00:02:00,333 --> 00:02:02,300
The real matter slowly surfaces

60
00:02:02,433 --> 00:02:04,133
That's tea's first layer of function

61
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:05,600
It doesn't solve the problem

62
00:02:05,933 --> 00:02:08,000
It gives the problem a chance to be spoken

63
00:02:08,166 --> 00:02:09,100
You'll notice

64
00:02:09,233 --> 00:02:11,733
what many relationships fear most isn't no result

65
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:14,133
but not even having the chance to sit down and talk

66
00:02:14,333 --> 00:02:16,000
When someone invites you for tea

67
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:17,566
it means at least one thing

68
00:02:17,733 --> 00:02:19,733
this relationship hasn't been shut down completely

69
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:21,400
When you're willing to go have that tea

70
00:02:21,633 --> 00:02:23,400
you're also expressing

71
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:25,833
I'm still willing to give this relationship some time

72
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,966
So the price of a cup of tea isn't in the leaves

73
00:02:29,133 --> 00:02:30,233
It's in the time

74
00:02:30,433 --> 00:02:33,433
A truly busy person willing to give you half an hour

75
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:35,700
that half hour itself is the cost

76
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:37,533
Someone who never lacks dinner invitations

77
00:02:37,566 --> 00:02:39,233
willing to sit down and hear you out

78
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:41,133
that act itself is a signal

79
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:44,233
The most expensive thing in a relationship is often not money

80
00:02:44,433 --> 00:02:47,033
but someone willing to put their attention on you

81
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:48,933
So tea is not a beverage

82
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:50,800
Tea is a very light invitation

83
00:02:50,933 --> 00:02:52,566
and also a very heavy signal

84
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:54,900
But tea only exchanging time isn't enough

85
00:02:55,100 --> 00:02:57,800
What really flows on the Chinese tea table

86
00:02:57,833 --> 00:02:59,033
is often face

87
00:02:59,166 --> 00:03:00,400
Think of a scenario

88
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:01,766
Someone pours you tea

89
00:03:02,033 --> 00:03:03,800
Do you feel nothing at all

90
00:03:03,966 --> 00:03:04,966
Usually not

91
00:03:05,133 --> 00:03:06,166
You might say

92
00:03:06,333 --> 00:03:07,133
Thank you

93
00:03:07,233 --> 00:03:08,766
You might tap the table lightly

94
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:10,833
You might push your cup forward a little

95
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:12,433
These gestures are tiny

96
00:03:12,633 --> 00:03:15,100
But they all express a kind of relationship order

97
00:03:15,333 --> 00:03:16,366
Who pours first

98
00:03:16,633 --> 00:03:17,533
Who gets poured

99
00:03:17,766 --> 00:03:18,633
How much is poured

100
00:03:18,833 --> 00:03:20,300
Where the cup is placed

101
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:21,433
Who sits in the main seat

102
00:03:21,633 --> 00:03:22,933
Who speaks first

103
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:25,000
These details look like politeness

104
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:27,233
but they're actually ranking the relationship

105
00:03:27,366 --> 00:03:28,633
What's more subtle is

106
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:30,600
many gestures on the tea table

107
00:03:30,833 --> 00:03:32,933
are never called transactions out loud

108
00:03:33,033 --> 00:03:34,700
You gift someone a box of tea

109
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,033
You wouldn't say

110
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:39,033
This tea is worth this much, so you owe me this much

111
00:03:39,233 --> 00:03:40,333
That would be too ugly

112
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:42,166
But the other person knows in their heart

113
00:03:42,366 --> 00:03:43,500
You put thought into it

114
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:44,833
You gave them face

115
00:03:44,933 --> 00:03:46,633
You took this relationship seriously

116
00:03:46,833 --> 00:03:48,933
That's the biggest difference between tea and money

117
00:03:49,166 --> 00:03:50,133
Money is too direct

118
00:03:50,333 --> 00:03:51,800
So direct it can hurt people

119
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:53,133
Tea is softer

120
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:55,500
Soft enough that both sides can keep their dignity

121
00:03:55,633 --> 00:03:58,166
So in Chinese social interactions

122
00:03:58,333 --> 00:04:00,766
tea often serves a very special function

123
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:03,166
It makes an exchange of interests not look like an exchange of interests

124
00:04:03,366 --> 00:04:05,800
Makes a request not look like a request

125
00:04:05,933 --> 00:04:07,800
Makes a gesture of goodwill not look like flattery

126
00:04:07,966 --> 00:04:10,333
Makes a rejection not too embarrassing either

127
00:04:10,533 --> 00:04:12,166
For example, someone invites you for tea

128
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:13,300
You go

129
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:14,900
That means you're willing to give face

130
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:16,033
You keep pushing back

131
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:18,033
the other person roughly understands too

132
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:19,933
this relationship hasn't reached that level yet

133
00:04:20,133 --> 00:04:21,966
Or say someone gifts you tea

134
00:04:22,233 --> 00:04:23,200
You accept it

135
00:04:23,333 --> 00:04:25,100
That's not just taking a gift

136
00:04:25,233 --> 00:04:26,900
It might mean

137
00:04:27,033 --> 00:04:28,900
you've accepted their goodwill

138
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,566
and implicitly agreed this relationship can continue going forward

139
00:04:31,733 --> 00:04:32,700
You don't accept

140
00:04:32,833 --> 00:04:34,300
It's not necessarily about disliking the tea

141
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:36,600
It might be telling the other person

142
00:04:36,833 --> 00:04:38,600
this relationship can't go any further

143
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:41,200
So the most interesting thing about tea is

144
00:04:41,533 --> 00:04:43,100
It rarely has a price tag

145
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:45,033
But everyone is valuing it in their hearts

146
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:46,933
That's the nature of social currency

147
00:04:47,033 --> 00:04:48,933
It might not be directly convertible to money

148
00:04:49,133 --> 00:04:50,333
But it can buy trust

149
00:04:50,433 --> 00:04:51,333
Opportunities

150
00:04:51,533 --> 00:04:53,033
A chance to be taken seriously

151
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:55,000
A graceful way out of an awkward moment

152
00:04:55,133 --> 00:04:56,933
If you only see tea as a beverage

153
00:04:57,133 --> 00:04:59,700
you won't understand many Chinese-style relationships

154
00:04:59,966 --> 00:05:01,300
Nothing was seemingly discussed

155
00:05:01,566 --> 00:05:02,933
But the relationship got closer

156
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:04,633
Nothing was seemingly promised

157
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:06,433
But an attitude was already conveyed

158
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:08,133
Seemingly only a cup of tea was had

159
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:11,200
But next time you ask, it won't feel so abrupt

160
00:05:11,233 --> 00:05:13,933
Now here comes the most important question

161
00:05:14,133 --> 00:05:16,233
Why should ordinary people understand this

162
00:05:16,333 --> 00:05:18,633
Because many people lose out in social exchanges

163
00:05:18,933 --> 00:05:20,300
not because they're unkind

164
00:05:20,433 --> 00:05:22,300
but because they misread the costs

165
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:23,633
Some people think

166
00:05:23,833 --> 00:05:25,766
I'm just having tea with someone

167
00:05:26,133 --> 00:05:26,933
No big deal

168
00:05:27,033 --> 00:05:28,900
But what you're paying might be time

169
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:29,933
Attention

170
00:05:30,300 --> 00:05:31,100
Emotional energy

171
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:34,133
And an opening that makes it hard to say no later

172
00:05:34,333 --> 00:05:35,500
Some people think

173
00:05:35,733 --> 00:05:37,800
They just gave me a box of tea

174
00:05:38,133 --> 00:05:38,933
No big deal

175
00:05:39,033 --> 00:05:40,966
But what they're really sending

176
00:05:41,133 --> 00:05:42,633
might be a relationship binding

177
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:44,033
Once you accept

178
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:46,966
saying no later becomes much harder

179
00:05:47,166 --> 00:05:48,700
Then there are people who think

180
00:05:48,966 --> 00:05:50,233
they're really good at networking

181
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:51,600
Drinking tea everywhere

182
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:52,900
Socializing everywhere

183
00:05:53,033 --> 00:05:54,366
Giving face everywhere

184
00:05:54,533 --> 00:05:55,900
Then they eventually realize

185
00:05:56,133 --> 00:05:57,633
They kind of know everyone

186
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:00,366
But not a single relationship can truly hold them up

187
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:02,300
That's the illusion in relationships

188
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:04,300
You think being busy means having resources

189
00:06:04,433 --> 00:06:06,633
Actually a lot of busyness is just drain

190
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:09,933
You think knowing lots of people means having connections

191
00:06:10,166 --> 00:06:13,233
Actually real connections aren't about who you know

192
00:06:13,366 --> 00:06:16,100
It's about who's willing to bear some cost for you

193
00:06:16,166 --> 00:06:18,133
at a critical moment

194
00:06:18,233 --> 00:06:19,533
That's a very real statement

195
00:06:19,633 --> 00:06:20,766
And a very useful one

196
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:22,333
To judge a relationship

197
00:06:22,366 --> 00:06:25,200
don't just look at whether someone says nice things

198
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:27,833
and don't just look at whether they've invited you for tea

199
00:06:27,966 --> 00:06:31,500
Look at whether they're willing to pay real costs for this relationship

200
00:06:31,700 --> 00:06:33,500
Whether they'll give you time

201
00:06:33,633 --> 00:06:35,433
Whether they'll help verify information for you

202
00:06:35,633 --> 00:06:37,700
Whether they'll say something fair about you

203
00:06:37,933 --> 00:06:39,333
When you're not in the room

204
00:06:39,566 --> 00:06:41,600
Whether they'll take on some trouble for you

205
00:06:41,766 --> 00:06:43,000
When you need it

206
00:06:43,133 --> 00:06:45,966
These are the real prices of a relationship

207
00:06:46,133 --> 00:06:47,400
Tea is just the entrance

208
00:06:47,566 --> 00:06:48,533
Not the answer

209
00:06:48,733 --> 00:06:51,133
So what ordinary people should be most wary of

210
00:06:51,233 --> 00:06:52,700
isn't not understanding social skills

211
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:55,900
but mistaking all the tea sessions, dinners, and small talk

212
00:06:55,900 --> 00:06:57,833
for meaningful relationships

213
00:06:57,866 --> 00:06:58,666
They're not

214
00:06:58,933 --> 00:07:01,566
Some tea leaves the relationship clearer

215
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:05,200
Some tea just adds another drain

216
00:07:05,400 --> 00:07:06,600
Some tea

217
00:07:06,600 --> 00:07:08,600
tells you this person is worth continuing with

218
00:07:08,933 --> 00:07:11,733
Some tea tells you to stay further away

219
00:07:11,833 --> 00:07:13,766
Relationships aren't about having more

220
00:07:13,966 --> 00:07:15,766
They're about seeing clearly

221
00:07:15,933 --> 00:07:18,433
What a cup of tea should really help you judge

222
00:07:18,433 --> 00:07:20,233
isn't whether their tea leaves are expensive

223
00:07:20,366 --> 00:07:23,733
but whether this relationship has real value

224
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:27,300
So finally, how should ordinary people make good use of this tea

225
00:07:27,533 --> 00:07:29,166
It's not about becoming smooth and calculating

226
00:07:29,366 --> 00:07:31,233
and not about learning to please people

227
00:07:31,433 --> 00:07:34,566
It's about learning to see three things clearly in relationships

228
00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:37,700
First, see what the other person is actually exchanging

229
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:40,333
Some invite you to tea to catch up

230
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:42,933
Some invite you to tea to collaborate

231
00:07:43,133 --> 00:07:44,400
Some invite you to tea

232
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:46,600
to test whether you can help them

233
00:07:46,833 --> 00:07:48,100
Some invite you to tea

234
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:50,533
just to offload their stress onto you

235
00:07:50,633 --> 00:07:52,966
Don't just listen to what they say

236
00:07:53,133 --> 00:07:56,633
Look at what they want you to do after the tea

237
00:07:56,833 --> 00:07:59,633
If after the tea you feel lighter

238
00:07:59,966 --> 00:08:01,533
this relationship might be nourishing you

239
00:08:01,633 --> 00:08:04,800
If after the tea you feel more burdened

240
00:08:04,966 --> 00:08:07,166
this relationship might be charging you

241
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:11,033
Second, see how much cost you're willing to pay

242
00:08:11,233 --> 00:08:12,833
It's not that relationships can't be maintained

243
00:08:13,033 --> 00:08:14,633
But you need to know your boundaries

244
00:08:14,833 --> 00:08:16,366
You can give time

245
00:08:16,566 --> 00:08:18,366
but you don't have to give commitments

246
00:08:18,533 --> 00:08:20,200
You can listen to them finish

247
00:08:20,433 --> 00:08:22,533
but you don't have to bear their consequences

248
00:08:22,633 --> 00:08:24,166
You can give face

249
00:08:24,300 --> 00:08:26,700
but you can't hand over your own judgment too

250
00:08:26,833 --> 00:08:29,900
Truly mature socializing isn't being warm to everyone

251
00:08:30,033 --> 00:08:32,600
It's knowing which relationships are worth warming up

252
00:08:32,766 --> 00:08:35,033
and which ones just need basic politeness

253
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:38,733
Third, turn tea into a tool for filtering relationships

254
00:08:38,900 --> 00:08:41,300
A cup of tea doesn't have to result in anything

255
00:08:41,433 --> 00:08:43,166
It can help you observe a person

256
00:08:43,400 --> 00:08:44,900
See if they only talk about themselves

257
00:08:45,133 --> 00:08:46,966
See if they respect your time

258
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:48,700
See if they can express themselves clearly

259
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:50,933
See if they're treating you as a tool

260
00:08:51,033 --> 00:08:53,233
Many relationships don't need a falling out

261
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:55,200
One cup of tea is enough to see clearly

262
00:08:55,366 --> 00:08:56,566
So back to the beginning

263
00:08:56,766 --> 00:08:59,600
Why can a cup of tea explain the Chinese social currency

264
00:08:59,933 --> 00:09:01,766
Because it was never just a beverage

265
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:03,766
It's a very gentle price quote

266
00:09:03,966 --> 00:09:05,400
Quoting time

267
00:09:05,566 --> 00:09:07,233
Quoting face

268
00:09:07,366 --> 00:09:08,800
Quoting trust

269
00:09:08,933 --> 00:09:11,366
Quoting whether you can still bring things up in the future

270
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:14,433
Someone who's good at tea isn't the most social person

271
00:09:14,600 --> 00:09:16,733
but the person who knows the price of relationships best

272
00:09:16,833 --> 00:09:18,400
They know when to sit down

273
00:09:18,633 --> 00:09:20,000
When to get up

274
00:09:20,233 --> 00:09:22,133
When to accept the cup

275
00:09:22,333 --> 00:09:25,000
When to gently push the cup back

276
00:09:25,166 --> 00:09:28,433
The most important thing at the tea table isn't how much you know about tea

277
00:09:28,733 --> 00:09:30,166
But whether you understand

278
00:09:30,333 --> 00:09:32,000
Some relationships are worth steeping slowly

279
00:09:32,200 --> 00:09:34,833
Some relationships should be put down after one sip

280
